<GuideLayout slug="how-to-choose-a-school-management-system">
    <p>
        A school management system (often called a school ERP) replaces
        registers, spreadsheets and WhatsApp groups with one place for
        attendance, marks, fees and announcements. Most systems look similar in
        a demo. The differences show up months later, in how easy it is for
        teachers and parents to actually use, who owns your data, and what the
        yearly cost turns out to be. This guide is a checklist to compare
        vendors before you commit.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Start with your problems, not a feature list</h2>
    <p>
        Write down the three things that waste the most time today. For many
        schools it is daily attendance, collecting fees and chasing receipts,
        and getting notices to parents. A system that does those three well is
        worth more than one with fifty modules nobody opens.
    </p>

    <h2>2. The modules most schools need</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Attendance</strong> marked by teachers, visible to parents.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Marks, results and report cards</strong>, with printable
            report cards.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Fees and receipts</strong>: fee structure by class, online
            payment, pending dues and payment history.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Circulars and announcements</strong> for parents and
            students.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Timetable and homework</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Staff attendance and leave</strong>, if you manage payroll
            inputs in-house.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Ask whether you can start with the basics and add modules later without
        rebuilding. A modular system lets a small school begin with attendance,
        marks and circulars, then add fees or report cards when ready.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Separate logins for students, parents, teachers and admin</h2>
    <p>
        Each role should see only what it needs. A teacher should see only the
        classes assigned to them. A parent should see only their own child.
        The admin creates and resets logins. Ask to see this in the demo, not
        just in the brochure.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Mobile access: installable web app or store app?</h2>
    <p>
        Parents will use their phones. There are two common routes. An
        installable web app (PWA) opens from the browser and can be added to
        the home screen, with no APK to download or update. A Play Store or App
        Store app gives a branded icon and native notifications, but costs more
        and must be published and maintained. For many schools a PWA is enough
        to start, and a store app can be added later. Ask which one you are
        getting and what a store app costs separately.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Data ownership and what happens if you stop paying</h2>
    <p>These are the questions vendors least like to answer clearly. Ask:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Who owns the domain name? It should be registered in the school's name.</li>
        <li>Can you export your student and fee data in a standard format?</li>
        <li>What happens if you stop paying the yearly fee? Does the system go offline, and for how long are your backups kept?</li>
        <li>Who publishes the store app, if there is one, and what happens to it if you leave?</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>6. Privacy and security</h2>
    <p>
        A school holds children's data. India's Digital Personal Data
        Protection (DPDP) Act has specific requirements for children's
        personal data, so ask how the vendor handles access, consent and
        deletion. Basics to confirm: HTTPS everywhere, role-based access,
        regular backups, and no student data used for advertising.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Notifications and messaging</h2>
    <p>
        Browser or app push notifications are usually included. SMS, WhatsApp
        and OTP messages are different: they are sent through third-party
        providers who charge per message. Ask whether those charges are
        included or billed separately at actuals, so there is no surprise
        later.
    </p>

    <h2>8. Pricing structure</h2>
    <p>
        Compare like with like. Some vendors charge a one-time build fee plus a
        yearly maintenance fee. Others charge per student per year. Neither is
        automatically better, but you need the three-year total, not just the
        first-year number. Our separate guide on
        <a href="/guides/school-management-system-cost-india/">school management system cost in India</a>
        explains the line items.
    </p>

    <h2>9. Setup, data entry and training</h2>
    <p>
        Someone has to enter your existing students, parents and teachers.
        Find out whether the vendor does it or your admin does, and whether
        there are step-by-step guides for each role. A system nobody has been
        trained on will not get used.
    </p>

    <h2>Questions to ask every vendor</h2>
    <ol>
        <li>Can I see attendance, marks and fee flow live, as a teacher and as a parent?</li>
        <li>What is the total cost over three years, including yearly fees and message charges?</li>
        <li>Is it an installable web app or a store app, and who publishes it?</li>
        <li>Who owns the domain and the data, and how do I export it?</li>
        <li>What happens to my system if I stop paying maintenance?</li>
        <li>How are new modules added later, and what do they cost?</li>
        <li>How long does setup take, and who does the data entry?</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
